Computational details The computational investigation of Belzutifan adsorption on silicon carbide (SiC) nanoparticles integrated density functional theory (DFT) and molecular dynamics (MD) simulations to elucidate both electronic and dynamic interactions. DFT calculations were performed using the OpenMX package 23 , which employs optimized pseudopotentials and localized basis sets for efficient large-scale simulations of hybrid organic-inorganic systems. The PBE-D3 exchange-correlation functional 24 – 27  was selected for its validated accuracy in modeling drug-nanomaterial interactions, combining the generalized gradient approximation (PBE) with Grimme’s D3 dispersion correction 28  including Becke-Johnson damping 29 . This approach is particularly suited for physisorption studies, as it accurately captures van der Waals forces that dominate drug-surface binding, with benchmark studies demonstrating mean absolute errors < 0.1 eV for adsorption energies of organic molecules on semiconductors 30 . The LCPAO-s2p2d2 basis set 25 , 31  was chosen for its optimized radial functions that provide superior convergence for SiC systems compared to standard Gaussian-type orbitals, while maintaining computational efficiency – tests show < 0.01 Å differences in bond lengths versus larger s3p3d3 sets for similar systems 32 . To model the nanoparticle environment, a hexagonal 4 H-SiC unit cell (64 atoms) was constructed with lattice parameters (a = 3.073 Å, c = 10.053 Å) matching experimental crystallographic data 33 , and surface termination followed the most stable (110) orientation as determined by previous DFT-MD studies 33 , 34 . The adsorption energy (E ads ) was calculated as: 1 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
